course: dictionary
lesson: 16017
title: Strong feelings in arguments
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Vehement is a word for very strong feeling, usually when someone is arguing or disagreeing. «The crowd made a vehement protest outside the town hall.» Protest tells you people were arguing, and the word vehement tells you they felt it very strongly.
explain: Look at the words around vehement. Protest, denial and opposition are things people say or do when they disagree. A «vehement denial» is a firm, forceful "no", not a quiet one. «He gave a vehement denial when they accused him.»
explain: Check your meaning by swapping in a simpler word. Try strong or fierce in the sentence: «He gave a strong denial when they accused him.» It still makes sense, so you have found the right idea.
fact: Vehement means showing very strong feeling.
fact: It is often used about arguing, protesting or disagreeing.
fact: «Vehement protest» means a loud, strong complaint.
fact: «Vehement denial» means a firm, forceful "no".
fact: In everyday speech, people often say strong, fierce or furious instead.
exercise: «The crowd made a vehement protest outside the town hall.» What does vehement mean here? | Very quiet and calm | *Showing very strong feeling | Happy and playful | Sleepy and tired
exercise: «Sam gave a vehement denial when he was accused.» Which word could replace vehement? | sleepy | gentle | silent | *fierce
exercise: Find and copy the word that means showing very strong feeling: «The vehement speech made everyone listen.» | speech | everyone | listen | *vehement
test: «His vehement opposition to the plan surprised the whole class.» What is the best meaning of vehement here? | Happy to agree with everyone | Quietly bored | *Strongly against, with great feeling | Very tired and sleepy
test: Jayden says vehement is just a fancy word for angry. Why is he only partly right? | Vehement means calm and peaceful | Vehement means tiny | Vehement is only used for animals | *Vehement is about strong feeling, which can be anger but can also be passion for a cause
exam: «The vehement protests lasted all week, but the council did not change its mind.» What does the sentence show? | *People felt very strongly and argued hard | The council agreed at once | Nobody cared about the council | The protests were quiet and short
